<h1>Jaxson Dart's Season-Ending Knee Injury: <em>A Historic Start, Cut Short</em></h1>
<div class="meta-row">
<span>📖 5 min read</span>
<span>🏈 Football</span>
<span>⚠️ Giants · Season-Ending</span>
</div>
<p class="dek">Jaxson Dart will have season-ending surgery on his left knee after tearing his MCL and PCL and damaging his meniscus in Week 2's loss to the Rams. His ACL is intact, and the Giants expect a full recovery in time for 2027 — but it ends, for now, one of the most explosive individual stretches to open a season in NFL history.</p>
</div><div class="content">
<div class="toc">
<div class="toc-label">In This Article</div>
<a href="#injury">What Happened</a>
<a href="#profile">The Athlete Behind the Numbers</a>
<a href="#outlook">What's Next for New York</a>
<a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
</div>
<h2 id="injury">What Happened</h2>
<p>Dart went down on New York's opening drive against the Rams, sandwiched between two defenders as he tried to release a deep throw under pressure, his left leg bending awkwardly underneath him. He walked off under his own power — an early, encouraging sign — but an MRI two days later revealed more extensive damage than the team initially feared: a torn MCL and PCL, plus meniscus damage. His ACL came back intact, which head coach John Harbaugh called a real silver lining, but Dart will still need surgery and is done for the regular season.</p>
<div class="stat-card">
<div class="stat-icon">🩹</div>
<div class="stat-title">Jaxson Dart — Injury Snapshot</div>
<div class="stat-sub">Week 2 vs. Los Angeles Rams · Left Knee</div>
<div class="stat-numbers">
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">MCL/PCL</div><div class="label">Torn, Meniscus Damaged</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">ACL</div><div class="label">Intact</div></div>
<div class="stat-num"><div class="num">2027</div><div class="label">Expected Full Return</div></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>Jameis Winston takes over as New York's starter for the rest of the season, with practice-squad arm Jake Haener promoted to backup. Winston struggled in relief against the Rams, going 11-of-27 for 111 yards with an interception, and the Giants have already signaled they'll explore bringing in more depth at the position.</p>
<h2 id="profile">The Athlete Behind the Numbers</h2>
<p>Dart is an unusual case for this kind of profile, because he never tested athletically at the combine at all. He skipped every drill except the throwing session, choosing to let his college tape speak for his arm talent and mobility rather than run a 40 or jump for scouts. What he did instead was simply keep producing: 1,498 rushing yards and 12 rushing touchdowns across his final three seasons at Ole Miss, on top of a school-record passing résumé that broke Eli Manning's career mark.</p>
<p>That dual-threat quality carried directly into his rookie transition. Before the injury, Dart had become the first quarterback in NFL history to open a season with at least three passing touchdowns, 50 rushing yards, and a 130 passer rating in the same game — a record set in Week 1 of this season. Two games in, he was averaging over 20 fantasy points a start, production driven as much by his legs as his arm.</p>
